    "content": "The 36th session of the PDRC Talks, organized by the Peace and Research Center, convened in Mogadishu this week where the Pro Dalxa political group publicly criticized Somalia's 4.5 clan-based power-sharing system, describing it as a festering wound on the nation. The group argued that the system, which allocates political representation among major clans, has perpetuated division and hindered effective governance since its introduction in the early 2000s.\n\nThe 4.5 system was established during the 2000 Arta Peace Conference as a temporary measure to balance power among Somalia's four major clans and minority groups. It has since been used in successive federal and regional administrations, but critics say it entrenches clan identity in politics and marginalizes smaller communities. Pro Dalxa, a political movement that has gained traction in recent years, is the latest to call for its replacement with a more merit-based and inclusive framework.\n\nKey figures at the forum included representatives from civil society, federal lawmakers, and members of the diaspora, who discussed the need for electoral reforms ahead of planned elections. The PDRC Talks, a recurring platform for national dialogue, have previously addressed issues such as federalism, security, and reconciliation. This session focused on the political system's shortcomings and potential alternatives.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the 4.5 system has direct implications. Many diaspora members are excluded from formal political participation due to the clan-based quota system, which often requires affiliation with a recognized clan to hold office or vote. Diaspora remittances also support clan-based networks, sometimes reinforcing the very divisions the system perpetuates. Calls to overhaul the system resonate with diaspora communities seeking a more inclusive and accountable government.\n\nThe Pro Dalxa statement adds to a growing debate within Somalia about the future of its political framework. While some defend the 4.5 system as a necessary safeguard for stability, others argue it impedes national unity and good governance. The outcome of these discussions could shape the country's electoral process and its relationship with its diaspora, who contribute significantly to the economy and development.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/pro-dalxa-oo-nidaamka-4-5-ku-sheegay-boog-ku-taalla-soomaaliya/427794/",
    "source": "horseedmedia.net",
    "source_name": "Horseed Media",
    "original_url": "https://horseedmedia.net/pro-dalxa-oo-nidaamka-4-5-ku-sheegay-boog-ku-taalla-soomaaliya/427794/",
