    "title": "Tree-Shade School Offers Education to Displaced Girls and Women Near Mogadishu",
    "original_title": "Displaced girls and women enjoy school under a tree outside Mogadishu",
    "slug": "tree-shade-school-offers-education-to-displaced-girls-and-women-near-mogadishu",
    "summary": "A free school under a tree in Garasbaley, outside Mogadishu, is providing education to 50 displaced girls and women, some for the first time.",
    "content": "In the Labaajo-boor neighbourhood of Garasbaley, on the outskirts of Mogadishu, a tree's shade serves as a classroom for 50 girls and women from displaced families. The students, ranging in age from eight to 60, attend free classes at the Fadumo Cureji Girls' School, which was founded by a young female university graduate. The school lacks walls and desks but offers instruction in reading, writing, mathematics and English to residents of the Nurshareco, Hawo Abdi and Weedow displacement camps.\n\nThe establishment of the school comes amid ongoing displacement in Somalia, where conflict and climate shocks have uprooted many families. The camps near Garasbaley host people who have fled their homes, and access to education is often limited, especially for girls and women. The founder, a recent university graduate, identified a need in her community and took the initiative to provide basic education.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this initiative highlights both the resilience of displaced communities and the persistent gaps in education for girls and women. Many in the diaspora maintain close ties to relatives in such camps and support efforts to improve access to schooling. The school's focus on older learners, including a 60-year-old woman, underscores how displacement can interrupt education for years and the importance of second-chance learning opportunities.\n\nThe story also reflects broader challenges in Somalia's education sector, where resources are scarce and many children remain out of school. Community-led efforts like this one often fill gaps left by formal systems, but they rely on limited resources and face sustainability concerns. The school's reliance on volunteer teaching and a lack of infrastructure illustrate the scale of need.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/11/displaced-girls-and-women-enjoy-school-under-a-tree-outside-mogadishu/",
    "source": "radioergo.org",
