    "slug": "radio-ergo-feedback-highlights-mixed-rainfall-impact-across-somalia",
    "summary": "Audience calls to Radio Ergo show beneficial rains in central and southern regions, but flooding and pest problems challenge farmers, with drought persisting in some areas.",
    "content": "During the week of 12-18 November 2026, Radio Ergo's nationwide audience feedback platform received calls indicating that rainfall had brought relief to many parts of central and southern Somalia, including regions in Jubbaland, Hirshabelle, and Galgadud. However, callers from Bari region in the north reported only light and insufficient rain. In Jowhar, Middle Shabelle, two callers said river flooding had inundated their farms. Many farmers, especially in the south, sought advice on controlling crop pests. The feedback also pointed to ongoing drought and livelihood losses in a few areas.\n\nThe mixed reports reflect Somalia's variable climate, where rainfall can be both a blessing and a curse. While the rains have improved pasture and water availability in some areas, they have also caused localized flooding, particularly along the Shabelle River. Meanwhile, pest infestations pose a threat to crops, and persistent drought in pockets of the country continues to undermine food security. Radio Ergo's platform serves as a vital link for rural communities to share concerns and receive information.\n\nRadio Ergo, a project of the UN's Food and Agriculture Organization, broadcasts via shortwave radio to reach audiences across Somalia, including remote and conflict-affected areas. Its weekly feedback reports provide a snapshot of the challenges faced by farmers and pastoralists. This week's calls underscore the need for tailored support: flood management in riverine areas, pest control guidance, and drought resilience in affected regions.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these reports offer a window into the realities facing relatives and communities back home. Many diaspora members are actively involved in supporting family members and investing in local agriculture. Understanding the mixed impact of the rains can help them target assistance effectively, whether through remittances for flood recovery, pest control measures, or drought relief. The feedback also highlights the importance of accessible information for rural communities, a gap that Radio Ergo helps fill.\n\nAs the situation evolves, continued monitoring and support will be essential. The diaspora's role in providing timely and appropriate aid cannot be overstated, and platforms like Radio Ergo facilitate informed decision-making. With climate variability expected to persist, investments in resilient livelihoods and early warning systems are critical to safeguarding Somalia's future.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/11/radio-ergo-audience-feedback-report-12-18-nov-2026/",
